{"parentUuid":"f95d8dec-00d0-4038-b0db-fc6b26c26dd9","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a7f5dba9e9d416cc6","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cg2QXJ9kFW2Gg6cYTr68Y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01U3B7JdpdqqES3YNE6UJrBu","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers\\docs\\vehicles\\ZASFAKPN5J7B88115\\sessions\\2026-10-07-session-record.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2292,"cache_read_input_tokens":90795,"output_tokens":400,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":112},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2292},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":400,"cache_read_input_tokens":90795,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2292,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2292,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01U3B7JdpdqqES3YNE6UJrBu":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers\\docs\\vehicles\\ZASFAKPN5J7B88115\\sessions\\2026-10-07-session-record.md"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"9e7a2a80-c4e3-405a-8a78-6e33b8d9bdf7","apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cg2QXHtrX2DRu1bLM8Qii","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"7e3e0fae-7590-4f14-9567-6110999ee41e","timestamp":"2026-10-14T15:59:01.466Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers","sessionId":"7cb02eb5-e299-4baf-b34b-9eb82aebbf17","version":"2.1.282","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-splashing-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"7e3e0fae-7590-4f14-9567-6110999ee41e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4f6844c0-3b82-41b2-99e4-abe62041f502","agentId":"a7f5dba9e9d416cc6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U3B7JdpdqqES3YNE6UJrBu","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Session record, 2026-09-26 to 2026-10-07 (saved 2026-10-07)\n2\t\n3\tVehicle: 2018 Alfa Romeo Stelvio 2.0T, VIN ZASFAKPN5J7B88115, about 142,290 km / 88,415 mi.\n4\tRepo: C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers (local main; remote `github` = https://github.com/dangelojohn/CUORE, remote `forge` is unrelated and never pushed to). cuore runs at http://127.0.0.1:5000 under the scheduled task \"CUORE Server\" (keep-alive, logs in C:\\ProgramData\\cuore\\logs).\n5\t\n6\t## 1. Diagnosis state (truthful, as of 2026-10-07)\n7\t\n8\t- EVAP codes P0455, P0440, P0456 are chronic. The system is physically sealed (three smoke tests) and every EVAP part is new (canister, ESIM, cap, filter, purge valve). The ECM calls a leak when it does not see the ESIM switch report sealed.\n9\t- 2026-09-28 scan: P0440 plus U0100, U1713, B1040, C141B, B1176 present; cleared. 2026-09-29 scan: P0440 and B1176 back; cleared 10:46 (SCAN_2609291046). 2026-10-04 engine session 11:16 and scan 11:15 read clean, but both came after the clear and before any monitor could re-run.\n10\t- Computed verdict: UNVERIFIED REPAIR. It flips to VERIFIED CLEAN only when a readiness read from the car (serial stream) shows the EVAP monitor complete with no code returned.\n11\t- Freeze frames: P0455 set at 93.7 % fuel, P0440 at 91.8 % (EVAP monitor runs only at 15-85 % fuel), P0456 at 80 %.\n12\t- Correlation finding: EVAP codes appeared in 12 sessions with no driver symptom reported; network and EVAP codes appeared together in 6 of 18 sessions (lift 0.75). The 28 Sep network/ADAS codes were gone by 29 Sep: one power or bus event. B1176 (rear left window riser) is a separate body fault.\n13\t- The MIL is not requested by these codes; \"no symptom\" is itself the expected pattern for EVAP.\n14\t- ECM software P235QB39 / 52170619. Recalls to check by VIN: 25V586000 (fuel pump), 18V636000 (2.0L ECM software). TSB 21-035-20 (TCM flash) applies. TSB 18-030-17 REV. B (PCM flash family includes the EVAP codes), S2125000002 (check the recirculation-line quick-connect first), 18-048-23 (small-leak verification needs wiTECH SLVT or Mode $06).\n15\t- Next decisive steps: wiTECH Flash tab comparison, then Test A (vacuum on the ESIM while watching the switch parameter). Do not clear codes until Test A is done.\n16\t\n17\t## 2. Pending items (priority order)\n18\t\n19\t1. wiTECH flash check; Test A; recalls 25V586000, 18V636000; TSB 21-035-20; stop clearing codes.\n20\t2. Maintenance from the 2018 US owner's manual (Mopar PDF, CONFIRMED): transfer case oil overdue (80k mi / 128k km / 8 yr); drive belt overdue (36k mi / 4 yr); air filter and spark plugs due at 90k mi; brake fluid every 2 yr; cabin filter 20k mi / 2 yr; coolant 150k mi / 15 yr; engine oil FCA max 10k mi / 1 yr (severe 4k), shop interval 8k mi / 12 mo. All \"unless records show it was done\": the cuore service ledger is still empty.\n21\t3. Enter a baseline maintenance visit and the 2026-09-15 oil change (MES reset, no odometer logged) in cuore.\n22\t4. Avery label print check (sample sheet on Desktop; 6576/6578 durable-ID margins unverified).\n23\t5. Rear left window riser (B1176); grey-cable (A6) read of the chassis bus modules.\n24\t6. Unsourced values: EVAP purge duty and vapour-pressure limits, peak boost, tyre pressures (door placard), P052E/P04DB PCV codes, coil-pack bolt torque, several plug torques (see UNKNOWN rows).\n25\t7. Known small bugs: mes/electrical_inspections.py `latest()` does not return the newest record after a second add (one failing check); the vehicle strip's \"...\" button is partly clipped at 400 px.\n26\t\n27\t## 3. What was built (all committed; GitHub main at cc07cd9 before the systems/electrical/job work)\n28\t\n29\tDossier redesign per MECHANIC_UX_REVIEW.md (verdict card, open-work checklists with shared tick state, collapsed counted sections, 104 px sticky strip, 16 px body, 44 px targets, dark toggle, logo slot at cuore/web/static/brand/logo.svg); Timeline (codes vs. what the driver notices, symptoms, \"what would you feel\" table, swimlane SVG with line-style grammar); Tools page (44 curated operations, workflows, writes stay MCP-only); Others' experience (22 YouTube how-tos, 11 browser-verified forum threads, 5 reference sounds); Media library (photos, scans, video, audio with sound tags, search, zip export); Parts catalogue (24 parts, sourced numbers, 9 verified images); Printable report and per-code PDF with print stylesheet; Feedback on every fact plus Inbox; Sibling cars (Grecale shares the Giorgio platform and GME 2.0T family, CORROBORATED; Levante is on the Ghibli-derived platform, component-level overlap only); Known-good bands (one-sided limits only become gauge bands); Routine maintenance area; Service hub; Gauges; Labels; launchers and keep-alive.\n30\tIn progress today (uncommitted at the time of this record): Systems layer (25 systems incl. PCV, dependencies, co-occurrence, Systems tab), Electrical layout (elements, per-code paths, inspections, Electrical tab) generalised to all systems (76 placed elements), Job/case workflow with hypothesis ledger, MES-style Modules view.\n31\t\n32\t## 4. Standing rules from the user (also in Claude memory)\n33\t\n34\t- Fable (the main model) is for decisions and review only; all building goes to low-cost subagents (Sonnet). Use half the tokens: 4-5 focused checks per feature, one screenshot pass, reports under 100-120 words, one suite run before each commit. Never Fable forks.\n35\t- Commit when agents finish; push to `github` only (user approves each push); never push to `forge`; never commit koch-mcp/.\n36\t- The mechanic makes the decisions and repairs; cuore supplies the most recent, truthful information with date, source and confidence; stale or unverified items are labelled; never invent values (UNKNOWN means \"use the service manual, TechAuthority\").\n37\t- Every error, family and job carries others' experience: verified YouTube how-tos and direct forum threads. Forums block plain HTTP checks (202 bot wall): verify through headless Edge over CDP.\n38\t- Images (photos, scans, borescope video) and audio clips are first-class evidence; parts need detail and images; printed pages must be properly formatted with an \"as of\" stamp.\n39\t- More interactivity: the mechanic can correct, ask, confirm or add input on any fact and see it answered.\n40\t- Systems (electrical, fuel, air, lubrication, cooling, EVAP, PCV, and the rest of the 25) are a correlational dimension for every issue; the physical layout (wiring, connectors, grounds, boxes, locations, conditions) is identified per problem, electrical first, then every system.\n41\t- CUORE is Alfa-branded first; later ported to Alfa, Maserati and Ferrari: keep marque specifics in data and config.\n42\t- Align cuore to a mechanic's technical tool like MultiEcuScan: one logical flow tying driver input, mechanic input, test results and malfunctions together.\n43\t\n44\t## 5. Methods and gotchas found\n45\t\n46\t- Headless Edge: CLI --dump-dom and --screenshot do not work on this PC; drive it over the DevTools Protocol (cuore/tests/check_live_widgets.py pattern) and kill it by its temp --user-data-dir (cuore/tests/_edge_cleanup.py), or 266 processes leak.\n47\t- Jinja: a dict key named \"items\" is shadowed by dict.items(); use bracket access or rename.\n48\t- cuore health endpoint is /api/health; the keep-alive identifies cuore by its health body; cmd /c needs extra outer quotes.\n49\t- Agent-driven `git push` is blocked by the permission classifier; the user approves a direct push in manual mode.\n50\t- MES language files hold only description fragments; the code-to-text map is in MES's encrypted database (left alone).\n51\t- Tests must set CUORE_STATE_DIR to a temp dir before importing cuore; media, feedback, symptoms, checklists, inspections and jobs are attested records, never car measurements.\n52\t\n53\t## 6. One-page sheets in this folder (pdf/, generators in pdf-sources/)\n54\t\n55\tDossier, Pending Items, Service Hub, Routine Maintenance, General Service Record, Oil Change, Brakes Wheels Tyres, Transmission and Drivetrain, Torque Settings, Drivetrain Torque Settings, Gauges, Service Labels, Sample Service Labels, Mechanic Notes, Vehicle Dashboard, wiTECH Test A (1 page and full), wiTECH Flash Check, ECM Flash Procedure, ESIM Bench Test, ESIM Wiring Test, ESIM Wiring Diagram Lookup, Recalls and Campaigns.\n56\t\n57\t## 7. Addendum (later on 2026-10-07)\n58\t\n59\tCommitted and pushed (GitHub main at the \"Handbook: architecture\" commit): Systems layer (25 systems) with Systems tab and by-system timeline; Electrical tab generalised to all systems (76 placed elements) with per-code physical paths and inspection records; Job workflow with hypothesis ledger (EVAP: ESIM signal path, EVAP: ECM calibration, network, each with real evidence refs); MES-style Modules view; the inspections `latest()` bug fixed. Handbook created at docs/handbook/ (decisions, architecture, knowledge tables, Stelvio case, methods, roadmap). All suites green at commit time. cuore restarted to serve the new tabs.\n60\t\n61\t## 8. 2026-10-08 decisions (saved as they were made)\n62\t\n63\t- Core directive re-stated after drift was flagged: diagnose this car on evidence, prove the fix, make the next same-model car start from what this one taught. EVAP is only the first sample; every one of the 25 systems and any code gets the same treatment. CUORE is the mechanic's diagnostic assistant, not an owner/manager tool: it advises and records, never blocks. Correctness over speed. One Stelvio at a time, start to finish, tools away, next car; no day board.\n64\t- Mechanic flow (12 steps) fixed: intake, complaint and symptoms, verdict, scan and codes, understand the fault, tests and inspections, hypotheses, plan (parts, tools), repair, verify, document, release. The Job page becomes the single scrolling workspace; other tabs are reference under More; a flow bar on every page says \"Suggested next: ... why\".\n65\t- Tools: CUORE recommends tools per step with sources, knows what the garage owns, shows the stage a tool is used (smoke test: after the quick-connect check and purge/vent actuation, before condemning canister/ESIM), supports the buy decision with sourced cost classes, records what was used and whether it was right, and tools away. For this Stelvio the decisive tools are a hand vacuum pump (Test A) and wiTECH, not a smoke machine (three smoke tests already sealed).\n66\t- Cases: closed jobs become cases keyed by model and family; a new car with matching codes gets the prior case's path, outcome, parts, tools, time and pitfalls, worded \"on the last Stelvio with these codes\", never \"proven\".\n67\t- Built or in progress today: flow state and API, flow bar and reordered tabs (partials), Start and Release (minimal), tools per step with inventory and tools-away, cases, any-code generalisation of fault tree, open work and hypotheses, dossier partials for the workspace, labels calibration and new label kinds (committed).\n68\t\n69\t## 9. 2026-10-08 completed (GitHub main at d5772aa and later)\n70\t\n71\tCommitted and live: Start and Release (minimal, never blocks), tools per step with owned/not-owned and the smoke-machine decision card, case memory seeded with this Stelvio's EVAP case, any-code generalisation (generic sourced fault tree, open-work cards and hypotheses for every system), the Job page as the single 12-step workspace with the flow bar and reordered tabs on every vehicle page, home opening the current car's job or Start, and the marque theme (dark, calm, hero band per car with an owner-supplied image slot, one accent action; data-marque alfa/maserati/ferrari). CUORE.md states the maxims. Remaining small fix: fold the page-level feedback panel into the FAB (in progress).\n72\t\n73\t## 10. 2026-10-08, later: review-driven work\n74\t\n75\tTwo more reviews saved in docs/research (MECHANIC_REVIEW, BENCH_UX_SPEC, JOB_UX_FIXES). Built: Bench landing page (resume card, next-action verdict sentence, 3 next actions with procedures, pass criteria, outcome capture, preconditions, live fuel gate and readiness, DTC chips with legend, accessible checkboxes), hero only on Bench (compact) and Report, 40px status strip elsewhere, step bar only on Job, Dossier collapsed with pinned jump bar, Systems graph-first, Codes scannable, feedback bottom sheet with three verbs, no truncation, single column, cache-busting, Live board (all channels, colour-graded, snapshots feed the hypothesis ledger), hypothesis rules (open -> supported -> confirmed only with pass/fail test evidence; refuted any time; evidence links, edit, soft delete, audit), test results on checklist rows, honest step states, suggestion dedupe, \"Live\" in the main tab row, English-only UI (language chooser removed as it did not work). Pending at compaction: the Job screen UX pass (no auto-navigation, header shows the viewed step, Next/Back buttons, compact phase stepper, step 7 layout, result sheet, confirm modal); its partial edits are on disk if uncommitted.\n76\t\n77\t## 11. 2026-10-08, evening: run-2 review and new surfaces (in progress)\n78\t\n79\tSecond mechanic E2E review saved as docs/research/JOB_UX_FIXES_RUN2_2026-10-08.md: items 1-7, 11, 12, 17 confirmed fixed; remaining R1-R8 (header buttons, job progress chip, row re-render after result, next test moving on, result-sheet meaning of Pass/Fail, stepper layout, confirm modal content, Undo/timestamps/chips, vehicle name on Job, step cross-refs, text size, touch targets) handed to the Job agent. Code cards: duplicate DTC removed on the Job step 5 card, system chips 44 px (landed). In flight on separate agents: Tests surface (/v/{vin}/tests, platform-wide mechanic test catalogue with sourced pass criteria, PASS/FAIL per test feeding hypothesis evidence via checklist results test:<id>), Systems dive-in phase 1 (/v/{vin}/systems/{key}: status strip, relationship ring with hot edges \"seen together on this car\", placed components, fault tree with Do it now -> Tests, hypotheses, this car's history and case memory, others' experience, what else to look at). Phases 2-4 planned: map coloured by car state with hot edges and a session time slider; unknown markers and inspection from the cell; live overlay and real-time DTC placement. Review note: the reviewer left test data on the real VIN (hypothesis \"Cracked EVAP purge line\", Fail 0.5 psi on row evap-5): delete before real work.\n80\t\n81\t## 12. 2026-10-13: review #3, Tell CUORE mobile, LAN\n82\t\n83\t- Mechanic re-test #3 (two reviewers, own temp-state servers): docs/research/MECHANIC_REVIEW_3_BENCH_JOB_2026-10-13.md and MECHANIC_REVIEW_3_TESTS_SYSTEMS_LIVE_2026-10-13.md. All findings applied (notably: the step-6 result sheet could open with no hypotheses and save an unlinked result silently; the job-progress chip needed an open visit; vehicle name regressed on Tests/System/Live; Live board Snapshot said success with zero channels). Suites green.\n84\t- Tell CUORE at /m/{vin}: owner-reported events (9 categories, 62 kinds, systems-tagged, \"owner-reported, possible link\" to codes within 14 days), Messages (mechanic asks + CUORE prompts via the feedback inbox), History, timeline lane; remembered free-text values (oil filter brand/part no., oil brand, station, battery/tyre brand), this car first then the garage. \"Mobile\" tab in the main row. Owner rejected the first design as a form: rebuild to the Ferrari benchmark in progress (dark, intro reveal on every open, 4 quick actions, 3-tap fill-up, camera on every form, Snap & fill).\n85\t- Photo reading: POST /api/vision/{vin}/read, provider CUORE_VISION_PROVIDER none (default, offline) | claude (opt-in, sends the photo to Anthropic) | tesseract (probe stub). Read values are suggestions the human confirms; saved with fields_meta[key]=\"photo\"; never car measurements.\n86\t- Media upload hardened for iPhone: magic-byte sniffing, 25 MB/413, EXIF date and orientation, GPS only with keep_location=true, HEIC stored with a null thumbnail (no decoder installed).\n87\t- docs/mobile/API_CONTRACT.md is the contract for a future Swift/Flutter app (Location Services, pump shortcut, push belong there); guarded by check_api_contract.py.\n88\t- LAN: launchers pass --host 0.0.0.0 (CUORE_HOST exists in config); PC is 192.168.68.80 (was 192.168.0.161; DHCP, may change); Windows Firewall rule for TCP 5000 still needs the owner (admin): New-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName \"CUORE 5000\" -Direction Inbound -Action Allow -Protocol TCP -LocalPort 5000 -Profile Any. The network profile is Public; no login on the app.\n89\t- Reviewer test data removed from the real VIN (hypothesis soft-deleted with audit; evap-5 result removed; .bak files kept). Report page 30 s -> 5 s (code_feel shared the workup cache); Job 3.2 s -> 0.3 s (flow_state cached, visit optional).\n90\t- Still on the owner: readiness read in the 15-85 % fuel window, wiTECH flash check + Test A, recalls, service ledger entries (need odometer, oil, filter, technician), TechAuthority values for the UNKNOWN specs.\n91\t\n92\t## 13. 2026-10-13 evening: FIRST LIVE READ FROM THE CAR (check-engine light on)\n93\t\n94\tAdapter vLinker FS r2 (STN1170 v4.3.2) on COM3, 14.1 V, engine idling 752-778 rpm, ECT 101 C, ambient 17 C. Three defects in cuore's live layer stood between us and the car, all fixed and tested tonight: (1) CAN-C behind the Security Gateway carries no broadcast traffic, so the passive-listen guard refused every read; an active Mode 01 PID 00 probe now verifies the bus; (2) this car answers legislated OBD only on 29-bit addressing (18DB33F1 -> 18DAF110 ECM, 18DAF118 TCM), not the 11-bit 7DF pair; reads follow the route that answered the probe; (3) STFAC on this STN firmware wipes the default pass filters so every reply was dropped (NO DATA); the session now re-issues STP and the header instead. Raw reads saved in sessions/2026-10-13-live-read.json; all stored as from-car observations.\n95\t\n96\tFacts read from the car (serial, 20:00-20:04):\n97\t- VIN confirmed ZASFAKPN5J7B88115. MIL ON. Stored: P0440. Pending: P0440, P0455. Permanent: P0440, P0456.\n98\t- Readiness since clear: ALL monitors complete, including EVAP. 1663 km and 8 warm-ups since the 29 Sep clear; 34 km with MIL on. Fuel now 20.8 % (inside the 15-85 % window).\n99\t- Freeze frame (P0440 / 18DAF110): fuel 28.6 %, ECT 63 C, 778 rpm, 2 km/h, 217 s after start, load 35.7 %, MAP 48 kPa, MAF 5.15 g/s, STFT +3.1 %, LTFT -2.3 %, lambda 1.007, purge commanded 18.0 %, throttle 13.3 %, timing -4.5 deg, baro 98 kPa, module voltage 13.5 V, ambient 17 C (IAT, vapour pressure, oil temp not reported in the frame).\n100\t- Mode 06 EVAP: gross leak (MID 39) PASS; 0.090\" leak (MID 3A) FAIL, 404 ms vs minimum 3965 ms; 0.020\" (MID 3C) one test FAIL 6096 vs limit 6000, others pass; purge flow (MID 3D) PASS. Catalyst, O2, EGR, VVT, misfire (1 count per cylinder) all pass.\n101\t\n102\tReading: the EVAP monitor has now run with fuel in window and FAILED the large-leak (0.090\") test -- the vacuum decay/hold time was 404 ms against a 3965 ms minimum. That is the first direct measurement behind the chronic P0440/P0455: either a real leak larger than 0.090\" that three smoke tests did not show, or the ESIM switch not reporting sealed (signal path / switch), which is exactly what Test A measures. Next decisive step unchanged: Test A (hand vacuum on the ESIM while watching the switch PID), then the wiTECH flash comparison. Do not clear codes.\n103\t\n104\t### 13b. Same evening: second and third live reads (module sweep)\n105\t\n106\tUDS works through the gateway on CAN-C. Modules answering: ECM (Magneti Marelli 10JA, 18DA10F1), TCM (ZF 8HP, 18), BCM (40), IPC (60), RFHUB (C7). DTCM/ESM/DASM: no address found by discovery (253 targets tried, 0 hits; they sit on other buses). Module codes with status flags (19 02):\n107\t- ECM: P0440-00 status 0xCD (confirmed, failed now, pending, MIL requested); P0455-00 0x4D.\n108\t- BCM: U1713-2F status 0x0F = ACTIVE NOW (testFailed + failedThisCycle + pending + confirmed) — a live network fault; U1765-86, U1736-87, B1176-97 (rear-left window riser), B10AA-4A: status 0x08 = failed since last clear, not failing now.\n109\t- IPC: U1736-86, U1736-87 status 0x0C (pending + confirmed). RFHUB: B1040-64 status 0x08. TCM: clean.\n110\t- ECM supports 46 Mode 01 PIDs; cuore decoded 25 tonight (the rest raw; decoders being added). IAT (0F) and oil temp (5C) are NOT supported by this ECM; IAT comes via PID 68. Module identities read (spare-part numbers: ECM 50544870, TCM 46337308, BCM 50554114, IPC 50551318).\n111\tSoftware built from this: active probe + 29-bit route + STN filter fix (landed); full-scan orchestrator, Scans area, scan health report generator, PID/Mode 09/Mode 06 decoders, UDS snapshot decoder, failure-type bytes, Live board channels, Test A in the Tests catalogue (in flight).\n112\t"}]},"uuid":"2d332a90-2523-4de7-b435-5befce1ed81f","timestamp":"2026-10-14T15:59:01.502Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers\\docs\\vehicles\\ZASFAKPN5J7B88115\\sessions\\2026-10-07-session-record.md","content":"# Session record, 2026-09-26 to 2026-10-07 (saved 2026-10-07)\n\nVehicle: 2018 Alfa Romeo Stelvio 2.0T, VIN ZASFAKPN5J7B88115, about 142,290 km / 88,415 mi.\nRepo: C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers (local main; remote `github` = https://github.com/dangelojohn/CUORE, remote `forge` is unrelated and never pushed to). cuore runs at http://127.0.0.1:5000 under the scheduled task \"CUORE Server\" (keep-alive, logs in C:\\ProgramData\\cuore\\logs).\n\n## 1. Diagnosis state (truthful, as of 2026-10-07)\n\n- EVAP codes P0455, P0440, P0456 are chronic. The system is physically sealed (three smoke tests) and every EVAP part is new (canister, ESIM, cap, filter, purge valve). The ECM calls a leak when it does not see the ESIM switch report sealed.\n- 2026-09-28 scan: P0440 plus U0100, U1713, B1040, C141B, B1176 present; cleared. 2026-09-29 scan: P0440 and B1176 back; cleared 10:46 (SCAN_2609291046). 2026-10-04 engine session 11:16 and scan 11:15 read clean, but both came after the clear and before any monitor could re-run.\n- Computed verdict: UNVERIFIED REPAIR. It flips to VERIFIED CLEAN only when a readiness read from the car (serial stream) shows the EVAP monitor complete with no code returned.\n- Freeze frames: P0455 set at 93.7 % fuel, P0440 at 91.8 % (EVAP monitor runs only at 15-85 % fuel), P0456 at 80 %.\n- Correlation finding: EVAP codes appeared in 12 sessions with no driver symptom reported; network and EVAP codes appeared together in 6 of 18 sessions (lift 0.75). The 28 Sep network/ADAS codes were gone by 29 Sep: one power or bus event. B1176 (rear left window riser) is a separate body fault.\n- The MIL is not requested by these codes; \"no symptom\" is itself the expected pattern for EVAP.\n- ECM software P235QB39 / 52170619. Recalls to check by VIN: 25V586000 (fuel pump), 18V636000 (2.0L ECM software). TSB 21-035-20 (TCM flash) applies. TSB 18-030-17 REV. B (PCM flash family includes the EVAP codes), S2125000002 (check the recirculation-line quick-connect first), 18-048-23 (small-leak verification needs wiTECH SLVT or Mode $06).\n- Next decisive steps: wiTECH Flash tab comparison, then Test A (vacuum on the ESIM while watching the switch parameter). Do not clear codes until Test A is done.\n\n## 2. Pending items (priority order)\n\n1. wiTECH flash check; Test A; recalls 25V586000, 18V636000; TSB 21-035-20; stop clearing codes.\n2. Maintenance from the 2018 US owner's manual (Mopar PDF, CONFIRMED): transfer case oil overdue (80k mi / 128k km / 8 yr); drive belt overdue (36k mi / 4 yr); air filter and spark plugs due at 90k mi; brake fluid every 2 yr; cabin filter 20k mi / 2 yr; coolant 150k mi / 15 yr; engine oil FCA max 10k mi / 1 yr (severe 4k), shop interval 8k mi / 12 mo. All \"unless records show it was done\": the cuore service ledger is still empty.\n3. Enter a baseline maintenance visit and the 2026-09-15 oil change (MES reset, no odometer logged) in cuore.\n4. Avery label print check (sample sheet on Desktop; 6576/6578 durable-ID margins unverified).\n5. Rear left window riser (B1176); grey-cable (A6) read of the chassis bus modules.\n6. Unsourced values: EVAP purge duty and vapour-pressure limits, peak boost, tyre pressures (door placard), P052E/P04DB PCV codes, coil-pack bolt torque, several plug torques (see UNKNOWN rows).\n7. Known small bugs: mes/electrical_inspections.py `latest()` does not return the newest record after a second add (one failing check); the vehicle strip's \"...\" button is partly clipped at 400 px.\n\n## 3. What was built (all committed; GitHub main at cc07cd9 before the systems/electrical/job work)\n\nDossier redesign per MECHANIC_UX_REVIEW.md (verdict card, open-work checklists with shared tick state, collapsed counted sections, 104 px sticky strip, 16 px body, 44 px targets, dark toggle, logo slot at cuore/web/static/brand/logo.svg); Timeline (codes vs. what the driver notices, symptoms, \"what would you feel\" table, swimlane SVG with line-style grammar); Tools page (44 curated operations, workflows, writes stay MCP-only); Others' experience (22 YouTube how-tos, 11 browser-verified forum threads, 5 reference sounds); Media library (photos, scans, video, audio with sound tags, search, zip export); Parts catalogue (24 parts, sourced numbers, 9 verified images); Printable report and per-code PDF with print stylesheet; Feedback on every fact plus Inbox; Sibling cars (Grecale shares the Giorgio platform and GME 2.0T family, CORROBORATED; Levante is on the Ghibli-derived platform, component-level overlap only); Known-good bands (one-sided limits only become gauge bands); Routine maintenance area; Service hub; Gauges; Labels; launchers and keep-alive.\nIn progress today (uncommitted at the time of this record): Systems layer (25 systems incl. PCV, dependencies, co-occurrence, Systems tab), Electrical layout (elements, per-code paths, inspections, Electrical tab) generalised to all systems (76 placed elements), Job/case workflow with hypothesis ledger, MES-style Modules view.\n\n## 4. Standing rules from the user (also in Claude memory)\n\n- Fable (the main model) is for decisions and review only; all building goes to low-cost subagents (Sonnet). Use half the tokens: 4-5 focused checks per feature, one screenshot pass, reports under 100-120 words, one suite run before each commit. Never Fable forks.\n- Commit when agents finish; push to `github` only (user approves each push); never push to `forge`; never commit koch-mcp/.\n- The mechanic makes the decisions and repairs; cuore supplies the most recent, truthful information with date, source and confidence; stale or unverified items are labelled; never invent values (UNKNOWN means \"use the service manual, TechAuthority\").\n- Every error, family and job carries others' experience: verified YouTube how-tos and direct forum threads. Forums block plain HTTP checks (202 bot wall): verify through headless Edge over CDP.\n- Images (photos, scans, borescope video) and audio clips are first-class evidence; parts need detail and images; printed pages must be properly formatted with an \"as of\" stamp.\n- More interactivity: the mechanic can correct, ask, confirm or add input on any fact and see it answered.\n- Systems (electrical, fuel, air, lubrication, cooling, EVAP, PCV, and the rest of the 25) are a correlational dimension for every issue; the physical layout (wiring, connectors, grounds, boxes, locations, conditions) is identified per problem, electrical first, then every system.\n- CUORE is Alfa-branded first; later ported to Alfa, Maserati and Ferrari: keep marque specifics in data and config.\n- Align cuore to a mechanic's technical tool like MultiEcuScan: one logical flow tying driver input, mechanic input, test results and malfunctions together.\n\n## 5. Methods and gotchas found\n\n- Headless Edge: CLI --dump-dom and --screenshot do not work on this PC; drive it over the DevTools Protocol (cuore/tests/check_live_widgets.py pattern) and kill it by its temp --user-data-dir (cuore/tests/_edge_cleanup.py), or 266 processes leak.\n- Jinja: a dict key named \"items\" is shadowed by dict.items(); use bracket access or rename.\n- cuore health endpoint is /api/health; the keep-alive identifies cuore by its health body; cmd /c needs extra outer quotes.\n- Agent-driven `git push` is blocked by the permission classifier; the user approves a direct push in manual mode.\n- MES language files hold only description fragments; the code-to-text map is in MES's encrypted database (left alone).\n- Tests must set CUORE_STATE_DIR to a temp dir before importing cuore; media, feedback, symptoms, checklists, inspections and jobs are attested records, never car measurements.\n\n## 6. One-page sheets in this folder (pdf/, generators in pdf-sources/)\n\nDossier, Pending Items, Service Hub, Routine Maintenance, General Service Record, Oil Change, Brakes Wheels Tyres, Transmission and Drivetrain, Torque Settings, Drivetrain Torque Settings, Gauges, Service Labels, Sample Service Labels, Mechanic Notes, Vehicle Dashboard, wiTECH Test A (1 page and full), wiTECH Flash Check, ECM Flash Procedure, ESIM Bench Test, ESIM Wiring Test, ESIM Wiring Diagram Lookup, Recalls and Campaigns.\n\n## 7. Addendum (later on 2026-10-07)\n\nCommitted and pushed (GitHub main at the \"Handbook: architecture\" commit): Systems layer (25 systems) with Systems tab and by-system timeline; Electrical tab generalised to all systems (76 placed elements) with per-code physical paths and inspection records; Job workflow with hypothesis ledger (EVAP: ESIM signal path, EVAP: ECM calibration, network, each with real evidence refs); MES-style Modules view; the inspections `latest()` bug fixed. Handbook created at docs/handbook/ (decisions, architecture, knowledge tables, Stelvio case, methods, roadmap). All suites green at commit time. cuore restarted to serve the new tabs.\n\n## 8. 2026-10-08 decisions (saved as they were made)\n\n- Core directive re-stated after drift was flagged: diagnose this car on evidence, prove the fix, make the next same-model car start from what this one taught. EVAP is only the first sample; every one of the 25 systems and any code gets the same treatment. CUORE is the mechanic's diagnostic assistant, not an owner/manager tool: it advises and records, never blocks. Correctness over speed. One Stelvio at a time, start to finish, tools away, next car; no day board.\n- Mechanic flow (12 steps) fixed: intake, complaint and symptoms, verdict, scan and codes, understand the fault, tests and inspections, hypotheses, plan (parts, tools), repair, verify, document, release. The Job page becomes the single scrolling workspace; other tabs are reference under More; a flow bar on every page says \"Suggested next: ... why\".\n- Tools: CUORE recommends tools per step with sources, knows what the garage owns, shows the stage a tool is used (smoke test: after the quick-connect check and purge/vent actuation, before condemning canister/ESIM), supports the buy decision with sourced cost classes, records what was used and whether it was right, and tools away. For this Stelvio the decisive tools are a hand vacuum pump (Test A) and wiTECH, not a smoke machine (three smoke tests already sealed).\n- Cases: closed jobs become cases keyed by model and family; a new car with matching codes gets the prior case's path, outcome, parts, tools, time and pitfalls, worded \"on the last Stelvio with these codes\", never \"proven\".\n- Built or in progress today: flow state and API, flow bar and reordered tabs (partials), Start and Release (minimal), tools per step with inventory and tools-away, cases, any-code generalisation of fault tree, open work and hypotheses, dossier partials for the workspace, labels calibration and new label kinds (committed).\n\n## 9. 2026-10-08 completed (GitHub main at d5772aa and later)\n\nCommitted and live: Start and Release (minimal, never blocks), tools per step with owned/not-owned and the smoke-machine decision card, case memory seeded with this Stelvio's EVAP case, any-code generalisation (generic sourced fault tree, open-work cards and hypotheses for every system), the Job page as the single 12-step workspace with the flow bar and reordered tabs on every vehicle page, home opening the current car's job or Start, and the marque theme (dark, calm, hero band per car with an owner-supplied image slot, one accent action; data-marque alfa/maserati/ferrari). CUORE.md states the maxims. Remaining small fix: fold the page-level feedback panel into the FAB (in progress).\n\n## 10. 2026-10-08, later: review-driven work\n\nTwo more reviews saved in docs/research (MECHANIC_REVIEW, BENCH_UX_SPEC, JOB_UX_FIXES). Built: Bench landing page (resume card, next-action verdict sentence, 3 next actions with procedures, pass criteria, outcome capture, preconditions, live fuel gate and readiness, DTC chips with legend, accessible checkboxes), hero only on Bench (compact) and Report, 40px status strip elsewhere, step bar only on Job, Dossier collapsed with pinned jump bar, Systems graph-first, Codes scannable, feedback bottom sheet with three verbs, no truncation, single column, cache-busting, Live board (all channels, colour-graded, snapshots feed the hypothesis ledger), hypothesis rules (open -> supported -> confirmed only with pass/fail test evidence; refuted any time; evidence links, edit, soft delete, audit), test results on checklist rows, honest step states, suggestion dedupe, \"Live\" in the main tab row, English-only UI (language chooser removed as it did not work). Pending at compaction: the Job screen UX pass (no auto-navigation, header shows the viewed step, Next/Back buttons, compact phase stepper, step 7 layout, result sheet, confirm modal); its partial edits are on disk if uncommitted.\n\n## 11. 2026-10-08, evening: run-2 review and new surfaces (in progress)\n\nSecond mechanic E2E review saved as docs/research/JOB_UX_FIXES_RUN2_2026-10-08.md: items 1-7, 11, 12, 17 confirmed fixed; remaining R1-R8 (header buttons, job progress chip, row re-render after result, next test moving on, result-sheet meaning of Pass/Fail, stepper layout, confirm modal content, Undo/timestamps/chips, vehicle name on Job, step cross-refs, text size, touch targets) handed to the Job agent. Code cards: duplicate DTC removed on the Job step 5 card, system chips 44 px (landed). In flight on separate agents: Tests surface (/v/{vin}/tests, platform-wide mechanic test catalogue with sourced pass criteria, PASS/FAIL per test feeding hypothesis evidence via checklist results test:<id>), Systems dive-in phase 1 (/v/{vin}/systems/{key}: status strip, relationship ring with hot edges \"seen together on this car\", placed components, fault tree with Do it now -> Tests, hypotheses, this car's history and case memory, others' experience, what else to look at). Phases 2-4 planned: map coloured by car state with hot edges and a session time slider; unknown markers and inspection from the cell; live overlay and real-time DTC placement. Review note: the reviewer left test data on the real VIN (hypothesis \"Cracked EVAP purge line\", Fail 0.5 psi on row evap-5): delete before real work.\n\n## 12. 2026-10-13: review #3, Tell CUORE mobile, LAN\n\n- Mechanic re-test #3 (two reviewers, own temp-state servers): docs/research/MECHANIC_REVIEW_3_BENCH_JOB_2026-10-13.md and MECHANIC_REVIEW_3_TESTS_SYSTEMS_LIVE_2026-10-13.md. All findings applied (notably: the step-6 result sheet could open with no hypotheses and save an unlinked result silently; the job-progress chip needed an open visit; vehicle name regressed on Tests/System/Live; Live board Snapshot said success with zero channels). Suites green.\n- Tell CUORE at /m/{vin}: owner-reported events (9 categories, 62 kinds, systems-tagged, \"owner-reported, possible link\" to codes within 14 days), Messages (mechanic asks + CUORE prompts via the feedback inbox), History, timeline lane; remembered free-text values (oil filter brand/part no., oil brand, station, battery/tyre brand), this car first then the garage. \"Mobile\" tab in the main row. Owner rejected the first design as a form: rebuild to the Ferrari benchmark in progress (dark, intro reveal on every open, 4 quick actions, 3-tap fill-up, camera on every form, Snap & fill).\n- Photo reading: POST /api/vision/{vin}/read, provider CUORE_VISION_PROVIDER none (default, offline) | claude (opt-in, sends the photo to Anthropic) | tesseract (probe stub). Read values are suggestions the human confirms; saved with fields_meta[key]=\"photo\"; never car measurements.\n- Media upload hardened for iPhone: magic-byte sniffing, 25 MB/413, EXIF date and orientation, GPS only with keep_location=true, HEIC stored with a null thumbnail (no decoder installed).\n- docs/mobile/API_CONTRACT.md is the contract for a future Swift/Flutter app (Location Services, pump shortcut, push belong there); guarded by check_api_contract.py.\n- LAN: launchers pass --host 0.0.0.0 (CUORE_HOST exists in config); PC is 192.168.68.80 (was 192.168.0.161; DHCP, may change); Windows Firewall rule for TCP 5000 still needs the owner (admin): New-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName \"CUORE 5000\" -Direction Inbound -Action Allow -Protocol TCP -LocalPort 5000 -Profile Any. The network profile is Public; no login on the app.\n- Reviewer test data removed from the real VIN (hypothesis soft-deleted with audit; evap-5 result removed; .bak files kept). Report page 30 s -> 5 s (code_feel shared the workup cache); Job 3.2 s -> 0.3 s (flow_state cached, visit optional).\n- Still on the owner: readiness read in the 15-85 % fuel window, wiTECH flash check + Test A, recalls, service ledger entries (need odometer, oil, filter, technician), TechAuthority values for the UNKNOWN specs.\n\n## 13. 2026-10-13 evening: FIRST LIVE READ FROM THE CAR (check-engine light on)\n\nAdapter vLinker FS r2 (STN1170 v4.3.2) on COM3, 14.1 V, engine idling 752-778 rpm, ECT 101 C, ambient 17 C. Three defects in cuore's live layer stood between us and the car, all fixed and tested tonight: (1) CAN-C behind the Security Gateway carries no broadcast traffic, so the passive-listen guard refused every read; an active Mode 01 PID 00 probe now verifies the bus; (2) this car answers legislated OBD only on 29-bit addressing (18DB33F1 -> 18DAF110 ECM, 18DAF118 TCM), not the 11-bit 7DF pair; reads follow the route that answered the probe; (3) STFAC on this STN firmware wipes the default pass filters so every reply was dropped (NO DATA); the session now re-issues STP and the header instead. Raw reads saved in sessions/2026-10-13-live-read.json; all stored as from-car observations.\n\nFacts read from the car (serial, 20:00-20:04):\n- VIN confirmed ZASFAKPN5J7B88115. MIL ON. Stored: P0440. Pending: P0440, P0455. Permanent: P0440, P0456.\n- Readiness since clear: ALL monitors complete, including EVAP. 1663 km and 8 warm-ups since the 29 Sep clear; 34 km with MIL on. Fuel now 20.8 % (inside the 15-85 % window).\n- Freeze frame (P0440 / 18DAF110): fuel 28.6 %, ECT 63 C, 778 rpm, 2 km/h, 217 s after start, load 35.7 %, MAP 48 kPa, MAF 5.15 g/s, STFT +3.1 %, LTFT -2.3 %, lambda 1.007, purge commanded 18.0 %, throttle 13.3 %, timing -4.5 deg, baro 98 kPa, module voltage 13.5 V, ambient 17 C (IAT, vapour pressure, oil temp not reported in the frame).\n- Mode 06 EVAP: gross leak (MID 39) PASS; 0.090\" leak (MID 3A) FAIL, 404 ms vs minimum 3965 ms; 0.020\" (MID 3C) one test FAIL 6096 vs limit 6000, others pass; purge flow (MID 3D) PASS. Catalyst, O2, EGR, VVT, misfire (1 count per cylinder) all pass.\n\nReading: the EVAP monitor has now run with fuel in window and FAILED the large-leak (0.090\") test -- the vacuum decay/hold time was 404 ms against a 3965 ms minimum. That is the first direct measurement behind the chronic P0440/P0455: either a real leak larger than 0.090\" that three smoke tests did not show, or the ESIM switch not reporting sealed (signal path / switch), which is exactly what Test A measures. Next decisive step unchanged: Test A (hand vacuum on the ESIM while watching the switch PID), then the wiTECH flash comparison. Do not clear codes.\n\n### 13b. Same evening: second and third live reads (module sweep)\n\nUDS works through the gateway on CAN-C. Modules answering: ECM (Magneti Marelli 10JA, 18DA10F1), TCM (ZF 8HP, 18), BCM (40), IPC (60), RFHUB (C7). DTCM/ESM/DASM: no address found by discovery (253 targets tried, 0 hits; they sit on other buses). Module codes with status flags (19 02):\n- ECM: P0440-00 status 0xCD (confirmed, failed now, pending, MIL requested); P0455-00 0x4D.\n- BCM: U1713-2F status 0x0F = ACTIVE NOW (testFailed + failedThisCycle + pending + confirmed) — a live network fault; U1765-86, U1736-87, B1176-97 (rear-left window riser), B10AA-4A: status 0x08 = failed since last clear, not failing now.\n- IPC: U1736-86, U1736-87 status 0x0C (pending + confirmed). RFHUB: B1040-64 status 0x08. TCM: clean.\n- ECM supports 46 Mode 01 PIDs; cuore decoded 25 tonight (the rest raw; decoders being added). IAT (0F) and oil temp (5C) are NOT supported by this ECM; IAT comes via PID 68. Module identities read (spare-part numbers: ECM 50544870, TCM 46337308, BCM 50554114, IPC 50551318).\nSoftware built from this: active probe + 29-bit route + STN filter fix (landed); full-scan orchestrator, Scans area, scan health report generator, PID/Mode 09/Mode 06 decoders, UDS snapshot decoder, failure-type bytes, Live board channels, Test A in the Tests catalogue (in flight).\n","numLines":112,"startLine":1,"totalLines":112}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7e3e0fae-7590-4f14-9567-6110999ee41e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers","sessionId":"7cb02eb5-e299-4baf-b34b-9eb82aebbf17","version":"2.1.282","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-splashing-comet"}
